        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>SNOMEDCT_US:27272007</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>UMLS:C0240635</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>Elevated palate</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>High arched palate</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>High palate</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>High, arched palate</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>High-arched palate</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>Increased palatal height</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>Palate high-arched</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>Palate, high-arched</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Ogival palate</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:id>HP:0000218</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:comment>The measuring device for this assessment is described in (Hall JG, Froster-Iskenius UG, Allanson JE, Gripp K, Slavotinek A. 2006. Handbook of Normal Physical Measurements. 2nd edition. Oxford Medical, publishers). A high palate is often associated with a narrow palate. However, a narrow palate can easily give a false appearance of a high palate. Height and width of the palate should be assessed and coded separately. We do not recommend the subjective determination because this term can be overused and applied inaccurately.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>High palate</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
